From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-snd: route up to twelve PCM channels to macOS output
 speakers

Snapshot the selected/default output's configured speaker layout when the
guest first reads device config. Advertise fixed mono-to-twelve-channel PCM
maps in ALSA order, keeping explicitly identified height speakers (5.1.2,
7.1.4). Do not infer 5.1.2 from an eight-channel count: unclassified/discrete
layouts use the preferred stereo pair. Capture remains mono/stereo. Correct
config.chmaps to match all entries, and the TSL..BRC chmap positions to the
Linux VirtIO/ALSA UAPI numbers (30..36; upstream had 34..40).

Use a device-bound HAL output unit. Default-output and device-list
notifications wake the pump to rebuild it at once (AirPods connecting, a
selected device unplugged); the layout is also rechecked once a second. The
callback consumes guest-format PCM on the existing ring/clock, then applies a
precomputed speaker matrix into interleaved Float32 physical outputs. Missing
speakers fold into front channels with headroom; unknown physical channels
stay silent. A device with more than 32 output channels (BlackHole 64ch,
aggregates, large interfaces) gets a stereo route without a channel map, the
HAL's default mapping as before. Rebuild/retry output units on hotplug
without changing guest frame accounting or allocating/locking in the
callback. The WAV diagnostic now captures guest PCM before mapping.

Guest maps remain fixed for a boot: virtio-snd/ALSA cache PCM capabilities and
there is no PCM-info/channel-map-change event. Restart the VM to expose added
channels or a new guest speaker layout. This is PCM routing, not Atmos encoding.

Tests: cargo test -p krun-devices --features snd --test macos_audio (layout
parsing, guest maps vs config, permutation/height routing, 7.1.4, downmix,
sample formats, UAPI numbers).
